# Lesson 8 of 10 — MySQL Replication, High Availability & Disaster Recovery

This is one of the most important DBA lessons.

By the end, you should understand how to design and operate:

```text
MySQL Source → Replica
GTID
Binary logs
Replication lag
Read replicas
Switchover
Failover
High Availability
Disaster Recovery
Replication troubleshooting
```

---

# 1. What Is Replication?

MySQL replication copies database changes from one MySQL server to another.

Basic architecture:

```text
             SOURCE
          MySQL Server
               |
               | Binary Log
               |
               v
             REPLICA
          MySQL Server
```

The source handles changes.

The replica receives and applies those changes.

---

# 2. Why Use Replication?

Replication can provide:

```text
Read scaling
High availability
Disaster recovery
Reporting
Backup support
Migration
Geographic redundancy
```

But:

> **Replication is not a backup.**

This distinction is critical.

---

# 3. Replication Is Not Backup

Suppose someone runs:

```sql
DROP DATABASE production;
```

on the source.

Replication may faithfully replicate that destructive operation.

Result:

```text
SOURCE
DROP DATABASE
      |
      v
REPLICA
DROP DATABASE
```

So:

```text
Replication
    ≠
Backup
```

You need both.

---

# 4. Basic Replication Architecture

```text
                 Application
                     |
                     v
                  SOURCE
                     |
              Binary Log
                     |
          +----------+----------+
          |                     |
          v                     v
      REPLICA 1              REPLICA 2
          |                     |
          v                     v
       Reporting             DR
```

This is a common pattern.

---

# 5. Binary Log

The binary log is central to MySQL replication.

It records changes/events that can be used for replication and point-in-time recovery.

Conceptually:

```text
SQL transaction
      |
      v
Binary Log
      |
      +------> Replica
      |
      +------> Backup/PITR
```

---

# 6. Check Binary Logging

Run:

```sql
SHOW VARIABLES LIKE 'log_bin';
```

You generally want:

```text
log_bin = ON
```

for a server intended to act as a replication source.

Check the format:

```sql
SHOW VARIABLES LIKE 'binlog_format';
```

Modern MySQL environments commonly use row-based logging.

---

# 7. Binary Log Files

You can inspect:

```sql
SHOW BINARY LOG STATUS;
```

Depending on your MySQL version, commands and output may differ from older tutorials.

You may see information such as:

```text
File
Position
GTID
```

These identify the current binary-log state.

---

# 8. GTID

GTID means:

> **Global Transaction Identifier**

Instead of relying only on:

```text
binlog-file + position
```

transactions can be identified using GTIDs.

Conceptually:

```text
Server UUID
     +
Transaction Number
     =
GTID
```

Example conceptually:

```text
aaaaaaaa-bbbb-cccc-dddd-eeeeeeeeeeee:12345
```

---

# 9. Why GTID Is Useful

Without GTID:

```text
Source
binlog.000123
position 456789
```

Replication configuration can depend heavily on exact binary-log coordinates.

With GTID:

```text
Transaction
      ↓
Unique identity
      ↓
Replica knows what it has already executed
```

This can greatly simplify replication administration and failover.

---

# 10. Check GTID

```sql
SHOW VARIABLES LIKE 'gtid_mode';
```

You may also inspect:

```sql
SHOW VARIABLES LIKE 'enforce_gtid_consistency';
```

A typical modern HA design deliberately plans around GTIDs.

---

# 11. Source and Replica Terminology

Older MySQL documentation commonly used:

```text
Master
Slave
```

Modern terminology uses:

```text
Source
Replica
```

Use the modern terminology in your DBA work.

---

# 12. Replication Flow

At a high level:

```text
1. Client writes to Source
          ↓
2. Transaction commits
          ↓
3. Event written to binary log
          ↓
4. Replica receives event
          ↓
5. Replica processes event
          ↓
6. Replica applies transaction
```

Conceptually:

```text
SOURCE
  |
  | Binary Log
  v
Receiver
  |
  v
Relay Log
  |
  v
Applier
  |
  v
Replica Data
```

---

# 13. Relay Log

The replica receives source events and stores them locally in relay logs.

Think:

```text
SOURCE
   |
   | binlog
   v
REPLICA
   |
   v
Relay Log
   |
   v
Apply
   |
   v
Database
```

The relay log is part of the replication mechanism.

---

# 14. Replication Threads

Conceptually, the replica has two major activities:

```text
Source
  |
  v
Receive
  |
  v
Apply
```

Modern MySQL exposes replication status that helps you determine whether receiving and applying are functioning correctly.

Use:

```sql
SHOW REPLICA STATUS\G
```

---

# 15. Important Replication Status

On a replica:

```sql
SHOW REPLICA STATUS\G
```

Pay attention to fields such as:

```text
Replica_IO_Running
Replica_SQL_Running
Seconds_Behind_Source
Last_IO_Error
Last_SQL_Error
Retrieved_Gtid_Set
Executed_Gtid_Set
```

Exact fields can vary by MySQL release.

---

# 16. Healthy Replication

A simplified healthy state:

```text
IO thread       = running
SQL thread      = running
Errors          = none
Lag             = acceptable
```

Conceptually:

```text
SOURCE
  |
  | continuously
  v
REPLICA
  |
  +-- Receiving ✓
  +-- Applying  ✓
  +-- Lag       Low
```

---

# 17. Replication Lag

Suppose:

```text
Source:
Transaction 1000

Replica:
Transaction 950
```

The replica is behind.

This is:

> **Replication lag**

---

# 18. Why Replication Lag Happens

Common causes:

```text
Heavy write workload
Slow replica disk
Insufficient CPU
Large transactions
Long-running queries
Lock contention
Network problems
DDL
Poor configuration
Replica overloaded by reporting
```

---

# 19. `Seconds_Behind_Source`

You may see:

```text
Seconds_Behind_Source: 25
```

This can be useful, but:

> **Don't treat it as a perfect measurement of replication health.**

Replication lag is more complicated than one number.

You should also inspect:

```text
GTID progress
Transaction backlog
IO state
SQL/apply state
Network
Disk
Locks
```

---

# 20. Example Replication Problem

Suppose:

```text
SOURCE
Writes: 10,000 txn/sec

REPLICA
Applies: 6,000 txn/sec
```

Backlog grows:

```text
4,000 txn/sec
```

Eventually:

```text
Replication lag
      ↑
      ↑
      ↑
```

The replica cannot keep up.

---

# 21. Reporting Can Cause Lag

Imagine:

```text
Application writes
      |
      v
Source
      |
      v
Replica
      |
      +--> Huge reporting query
```

The reporting query consumes:

```text
CPU
Memory
Disk
Locks
```

and replication may slow down.

This is why read replicas aren't automatically free capacity.

---

# 22. Read Scaling

Suppose application workload is:

```text
80% reads
20% writes
```

You could potentially use:

```text
               Application
                /       \
              Write     Read
                |         |
                v         v
             Source    Replica
```

This can reduce read pressure on the source.

But your application must correctly handle:

* Read-after-write consistency
* Replica lag
* Transactions
* Routing

---

# 23. Read-After-Write Problem

User performs:

```text
INSERT order
    ↓
Source
```

Immediately:

```text
SELECT order
    ↓
Replica
```

If replication hasn't caught up:

```text
Order not found
```

The application may think the insert failed.

This is a classic distributed-systems issue.

---

# 24. Read Routing

A better design may use:

```text
WRITE
  ↓
SOURCE

CRITICAL READ
  ↓
SOURCE

EVENTUAL READ
  ↓
REPLICA
```

Not every query should automatically go to a replica.

---

# 25. Read-Only Replicas

For a reporting replica, you may configure it to prevent accidental writes.

Check:

```sql
SHOW VARIABLES LIKE 'read_only';
```

Also understand:

```sql
SHOW VARIABLES LIKE 'super_read_only';
```

These settings have important operational implications.

---

# 26. `super_read_only`

`super_read_only` provides stronger protection against accidental writes by privileged users.

Conceptually:

```text
Application
     |
     X
Replica writes blocked
```

This can help protect replicas.

But administrative operations and replication behavior have special considerations.

Always understand exactly what you're changing before enabling/disabling it.

---

# 27. Replication Security

Replication requires authentication between servers.

You should use:

```text
Dedicated replication account
Strong authentication
TLS where appropriate
Least privilege
Restricted network access
```

Don't use:

```text
root
```

as your replication account.

---

# 28. Network Security

Example:

```text
Source
10.0.1.10
    |
    | TCP 3306
    |
    v
Replica
10.0.2.10
```

Firewall rules should allow only the required communication.

Don't expose MySQL unnecessarily to the internet.

---

# 29. TLS

For sensitive environments, replication traffic should be protected appropriately.

Conceptually:

```text
Source
  |
  | Encrypted replication
  v
Replica
```

This protects replication traffic from network-level interception.

---

# 30. High Availability vs Replication

This distinction is extremely important.

Replication:

```text
Source → Replica
```

doesn't automatically provide:

```text
Automatic failover
Traffic management
Application routing
Leader election
Failure detection
```

Therefore:

> **Replication is a building block for HA, not necessarily HA by itself.**

---

# 31. High Availability

HA means the system is designed to remain available despite certain failures.

Conceptually:

```text
                Application
                     |
                HA Endpoint
                     |
              +------+------+
              |             |
           MySQL A       MySQL B
            Active        Standby
```

If A fails:

```text
A
X

B
↓
Active
```

---

# 32. Failover vs Switchover

### Failover

Unplanned failure:

```text
Primary
   X
   ↓
Replica becomes primary
```

### Switchover

Planned role change:

```text
Primary
   ↓
Controlled transition
   ↓
Replica becomes primary
```

Example:

```text
Maintenance
    ↓
Switchover
    ↓
Patch old primary
```

---

# 33. Switchover Is Safer

If you know:

```text
Tomorrow 2 AM
```

you can prepare.

You can:

```text
Stop writes
Check replication
Confirm target is caught up
Promote target
Redirect application
```

Failover happens when something has already gone wrong.

---

# 34. Planned Switchover

Conceptually:

```text
PRIMARY A
   |
   | Replication
   v
REPLICA B
```

Before switch:

```text
A = Primary
B = Replica
```

After:

```text
B = Primary
A = Replica
```

This is useful for:

```text
Maintenance
Patching
Hardware replacement
OS migration
Datacenter maintenance
```

---

# 35. Failover Scenario

Imagine:

```text
Application
     |
     v
Primary
     X
```

You need:

```text
Replica
   ↓
Promote
   ↓
Application redirected
```

But promotion isn't the only problem.

You also need to consider:

```text
DNS
Connection pools
Application configuration
Writes
Old primary recovery
Replication topology
Data consistency
```

---

# 36. Split Brain

One of the most dangerous HA scenarios.

Imagine:

```text
Node A thinks:
"I am Primary."

Node B thinks:
"I am Primary."
```

Now both accept writes.

```text
Application A
     |
     v
Primary A
     ↑
     |
  writes

Application B
     |
     v
Primary B
     ↑
     |
  writes
```

You can create divergent data.

This is:

> **Split brain**

HA systems need mechanisms to prevent or minimize this risk.

---

# 37. Why Fencing Matters

When a primary is considered dead, you need confidence it isn't still serving traffic.

Conceptually:

```text
Old Primary
    |
    X
Fence / isolate
    |
    v
Promote Replica
```

Otherwise:

```text
Old Primary
    +
New Primary
    =
Potential split brain
```

---

# 38. DR — Disaster Recovery

DR is broader than HA.

HA handles things like:

```text
Server failure
```

DR considers:

```text
Datacenter loss
Region loss
Major corruption
Ransomware
Human error
Infrastructure destruction
```

Example:

```text
Primary DC
     |
     | Replication/backup
     v
DR DC
```

---

# 39. RPO

RPO:

> **Recovery Point Objective**

It answers:

> How much data can the business afford to lose?

Example:

```text
RPO = 5 minutes
```

Means the recovery strategy should aim to lose no more than approximately five minutes of data under the defined failure scenario.

---

# 40. RTO

RTO:

> **Recovery Time Objective**

It answers:

> How quickly must service be restored?

Example:

```text
RTO = 30 minutes
```

Your DR architecture must be designed around this requirement.

---

# 41. RPO/RTO Example

Business says:

```text
RPO = 1 minute
RTO = 15 minutes
```

Your architecture must support those targets.

A solution requiring:

```text
8 hours to restore
```

doesn't meet the RTO.

A solution that loses:

```text
2 hours of data
```

doesn't meet the RPO.

---

# 42. Backup + Replication + DR

A mature environment may have:

```text
                 SOURCE
                    |
          +---------+---------+
          |                   |
          v                   v
      REPLICA             BACKUP
          |                   |
          v                   v
       HA/DR              Offsite
```

Each solves different problems.

```text
Replication → availability
Backup      → recovery
Offsite     → disaster protection
```

---

# 43. Disaster Recovery Example

Primary:

```text
New Orleans DC
```

DR:

```text
Dallas DC
```

Conceptually:

```text
Production
    |
    | Replication
    v
DR MySQL
```

If the primary site is lost:

```text
Production DC
     X

DR DC
  ↓
Promote
  ↓
Application
```

The exact architecture depends on network, latency, business requirements, and operational tooling.

---

# 44. Replication Failure — Scenario 1

You run:

```sql
SHOW REPLICA STATUS\G
```

and see:

```text
Replica_IO_Running: No
Replica_SQL_Running: Yes
```

Interpretation:

```text
Replica is applying existing events
but isn't receiving new events.
```

Potential causes:

```text
Network problem
Source unavailable
Authentication failure
TLS issue
Source binary log unavailable
Replication configuration problem
```

Check:

```text
Last_IO_Error
```

---

# 45. Replication Failure — Scenario 2

Suppose:

```text
Replica_IO_Running: Yes
Replica_SQL_Running: No
```

Now:

```text
Receiving = working
Applying = stopped
```

Potential causes:

```text
SQL error
Duplicate key
Missing object
Permission issue
Data inconsistency
DDL problem
```

Check:

```text
Last_SQL_Error
```

---

# 46. Example SQL Error

Suppose replication stops because:

```text
Duplicate entry
```

Don't immediately run:

```text
"Skip the error"
```

This is dangerous.

You first need to determine:

```text
Why did source and replica diverge?
```

Skipping transactions can make corruption/divergence worse.

---

# 47. Never Use "Skip Errors" as a Default Fix

Bad DBA behavior:

```text
Replication stopped
       ↓
Skip transaction
       ↓
Start replication
       ↓
Problem solved!
```

No.

Correct:

```text
Replication stopped
       ↓
Investigate
       ↓
Determine cause
       ↓
Assess data consistency
       ↓
Repair appropriately
       ↓
Resume
```

---

# 48. Replication Monitoring

Monitor:

```text
Replication state
Lag
GTID progress
SQL errors
IO errors
Network
Disk
CPU
Transactions
```

For production, create alerts for:

```text
Replication stopped
Replication lag exceeds threshold
Disk nearly full
Binary logs at risk
```

---

# 49. Disk Space and Replication

A very dangerous situation:

```text
Replica disk
    ↓
95%
    ↓
Replication continues
    ↓
100%
    ↓
MySQL/replication problems
```

Monitor:

```bash
df -h
```

on Linux.

On Windows, monitor the volume containing:

```text
Data
Logs
Binary logs
Relay logs
```

---

# 50. Binary Log Retention

Binary logs consume disk.

You need an appropriate retention policy.

Too short:

```text
Required recovery logs disappear
```

Too long:

```text
Disk fills
```

So:

```text
Retention
     =
Recovery requirements
+
Replication requirements
+
Available storage
```

---

# 51. Replication and Backups

A good architecture can be:

```text
Primary
   |
   +---- Replica
   |
   +---- Backup
```

But backup can also be performed from an appropriate replica depending on the architecture.

Advantages:

```text
Reduced source load
```

But you must ensure:

```text
Backup consistency
Replication health
Backup completeness
```

---

# 52. HA Testing

You don't have HA because a diagram says:

```text
Primary
Replica
```

You have HA when you've tested:

```text
Primary failure
     ↓
Detection
     ↓
Promotion
     ↓
Application reconnection
     ↓
Data validation
```

Do this in a controlled test environment first.

---

# 53. Disaster Recovery Testing

At least periodically:

```text
Simulate disaster
       ↓
Recover database
       ↓
Recover application
       ↓
Validate data
       ↓
Measure RTO
       ↓
Measure RPO
```

If your stated RTO is:

```text
30 minutes
```

but the last test took:

```text
2 hours
```

your DR plan doesn't meet its requirement.

---

# 54. Complete HA Architecture

A mature architecture might look like:

```text
                 Application
                      |
                HA Endpoint
                      |
              +-------+-------+
              |               |
         MySQL Primary    MySQL Replica
              |               |
              |               |
              +-------+-------+
                      |
                 Backup System
                      |
                      v
                 Offsite/DR
```

In larger environments, a dedicated HA/orchestration layer may manage:

```text
Health checks
Leader election
Promotion
Routing
Fencing
Topology
```

---

# 55. MySQL InnoDB Cluster

For modern MySQL HA, you should know the concept of **InnoDB Cluster**.

It uses MySQL Group Replication and MySQL Router to build a managed HA architecture.

Conceptually:

```text
          Application
               |
        MySQL Router
               |
       +-------+-------+
       |       |       |
      DB1     DB2     DB3
       |       |       |
       +-------+-------+
        Group Replication
```

This is more sophisticated than simply configuring one source and one replica.

---

# 56. Group Replication

Group Replication allows multiple MySQL servers to participate in a group.

Conceptually:

```text
        +--------+
        | Node 1 |
        +--------+
           /  \
          /    \
         /      \
+--------+      +--------+
| Node 2 |------| Node 3 |
+--------+      +--------+
```

The group coordinates membership and transaction replication.

It forms the foundation of several MySQL HA architectures.

---

# 57. MySQL Router

Applications ideally shouldn't need to know:

```text
Which node is currently primary?
```

Instead:

```text
Application
     |
     v
MySQL Router
     |
     +----> Primary
     |
     +----> Read replicas
```

Router can help direct connections according to the topology.

This simplifies application-side failover handling.

---

# 58. HA vs Read Scaling

Don't confuse:

```text
Read Scaling
```

with:

```text
High Availability
```

Example:

```text
Primary
   |
   +-- Replica 1
   +-- Replica 2
   +-- Replica 3
```

This may improve read capacity.

But if the primary dies and nobody can promote/re-route:

```text
Availability = still poor
```

HA requires operational failover capability.

